\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Semantic PER with the failure-direction signal. The semantic weight $w_i$ is set once per slot at line~9 and persists until the slot is overwritten by the ring buffer. Line~13's IS weights use the PER proposal $\mu _P$ (i.e., the sum-tree's $\mu (i_j)$), \emph  {not} the strictly-correct $\mu _{\text  {Sem}}$ that includes $w_{\text  {sem}}$ in the denominator; see Section~\ref {sec:theory} and Appendix~\ref {app:is-derivation} for the under-correction analysis and the bias bound of Eq.~\eqref  {eq:bias-bound}. The two-pass priority update (lines~10 and 17) is necessary because line~17 refreshes only the slots seen in the current minibatch, whereas line~10 must touch every slot whose semantic weight changed. The frame-to-step formula at line~7 is a linear interpolation $\hat  k/(K\!-\!1)\times (T\!-\!1)$; with $K\!=\!5$ keyframes and $T\!=\!50$ steps, keyframe~0 maps to step~0 and keyframe~4 maps to step~49.}}{22}{figure.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{alg:sem-per}{{1}{22}{Semantic PER with the failure-direction signal. The semantic weight $w_i$ is set once per slot at line~9 and persists until the slot is overwritten by the ring buffer. Line~13's IS weights use the PER proposal $\mu _P$ (i.e., the sum-tree's $\mu (i_j)$), \emph {not} the strictly-correct $\mu _{\text {Sem}}$ that includes $w_{\text {sem}}$ in the denominator; see Section~\ref {sec:theory} and Appendix~\ref {app:is-derivation} for the under-correction analysis and the bias bound of Eq.~\eqref {eq:bias-bound}. The two-pass priority update (lines~10 and 17) is necessary because line~17 refreshes only the slots seen in the current minibatch, whereas line~10 must touch every slot whose semantic weight changed. The frame-to-step formula at line~7 is a linear interpolation $\hat k/(K\!-\!1)\times (T\!-\!1)$; with $K\!=\!5$ keyframes and $T\!=\!50$ steps, keyframe~0 maps to step~0 and keyframe~4 maps to step~49}{figure.10}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{FetchPickAndPlace --- ballistic vs.\ argmin precedence.}{22}{section*.44}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces VLM-Verified Counterfactual Hindsight (Verify-CF). The teleport gate (line~5) is empirically necessary on planar tasks (FetchPush, 67\% teleport rate pre-gate on GPT-4o) but structurally redundant on the \textsc  {action} variant, which emits no position field --- a structural design choice we make explicit in line~5's variant check. Lines~3 and 8 implement the ``simulator-fork'' that gives the verifier zero modeling error: the verifier env's transition is the ground-truth env's transition by construction. Per-call CPU cost is 17.6\,ms (lines~3 + 8 + 9 together); the dominant cost is the VLM call at line~4 ($\approx \!4$\,s for GPT-4o, $\approx \!11$\,s for Opus~4.7). The procedure is invoked at the HER relabel step of any SAC+HER trainer once per failed episode.}}{23}{figure.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{alg:verified-cf}{{2}{23}{VLM-Verified Counterfactual Hindsight (Verify-CF). The teleport gate (line~5) is empirically necessary on planar tasks (FetchPush, 67\% teleport rate pre-gate on GPT-4o) but structurally redundant on the \textsc {action} variant, which emits no position field --- a structural design choice we make explicit in line~5's variant check. Lines~3 and 8 implement the ``simulator-fork'' that gives the verifier zero modeling error: the verifier env's transition is the ground-truth env's transition by construction. Per-call CPU cost is 17.6\,ms (lines~3 + 8 + 9 together); the dominant cost is the VLM call at line~4 ($\approx \!4$\,s for GPT-4o, $\approx \!11$\,s for Opus~4.7). The procedure is invoked at the HER relabel step of any SAC+HER trainer once per failed episode}{figure.11}{}}
